  "step_1": "The decision reason can be applied to the decision problem. The reason focuses on the sum of all pairwise differences between the outcomes of the two lotteries. To compute this, we compare each outcome of Lottery A with each outcome of Lottery B, considering their values (not probabilities), and sum the differences (A - B) across all pairs. Lottery A has outcomes: 4000, 2000, 0, 0 (since 50% for 0 is two 25% segments for comparison consistency). Lottery B has outcomes: 6000, 0, 0, 0 (25% for 6000 and three 25% segments for 0 to match 100%). Pairwise differences (A - B) across all 4x4 = 16 pairs include: (4000-6000), (4000-0), (4000-0), ..., (0-6000), (0-0), etc. The sum of these differences would be negative overall, as A’s outcomes are mostly smaller than B’s 6000. However, the reason prefers the lottery with the more favorable (i.e., higher) sum of outcome differences. Since B has a larger outcome (6000) and A has more moderate outcomes, the sum of A - B differences is likely negative, implying the reason would favor B. But the reason is defined structurally and can be applied.",
